== ONE PARTY, TWO FILES, against live project uccixoonmbhrnyczyigt
== 23 September 2026, rolled back. RUN TWICE, ON PURPOSE.

-- RUN ONE, BEFORE THE FIX. It found the defect.

  ERROR:  P0001: PROBE FAILED. One party can file only ONE file per agreement.
  The second answered {"status": "duplicate"}.
  escrow_evidence_one_fact_per_party is UNIQUE NULLS NOT DISTINCT and a file
  row has fact = null.

THE CONSTRAINT'S NAME SAID FACTS AND ITS BEHAVIOUR SAID FILES TOO. A file row
carries `fact = null`, and `nulls not distinct` makes two nulls equal, so the
second file any party ever uploaded to an agreement came back `duplicate` and
the product would have rendered "You have already filed that, so nothing was
added twice." to somebody who had just chosen a different photograph. Their
bytes would already have been in the bucket with no row pointing at them.

NOTHING COULD HAVE SEEN THIS BEFORE TODAY. The table, the constraints, the
trigger and the server action all shipped on 22 September with no client that
could pick a file, so no second file was ever filed. It was not caught by
reading the migration either: the constraint reads correctly if you read its
name, and `nulls not distinct` is four words at the end of a line.

-- THE FIX. 20260923092713 replaces the table constraint with a partial unique
-- index carrying `where kind = 'fact'`. Inside that predicate `fact` is
-- already `not null` by the `escrow_evidence_fact_is_a_fact` check, so null
-- semantics stop mattering rather than being relied on the other way round.

-- RUN TWO, AFTER THE FIX.

  ERROR:  P0001: PROBE ALL PASS (rolled back). Two files from one party
  (ok, ok), one from the other (ok), one fact (ok), the same fact again refused
  as duplicate. 4 rows filed, 0 kept.

The last assertion is the one that matters as much as the fix: the behaviour
the constraint was actually written for, one party stating one fact once, still
refuses. A fix that had simply dropped the constraint would have passed the
first four checks and lost that.
